  urldate = {2026-03-16},
  abstract = {This paper presents an investigation into the positions of political parties on European Union (EU) solidarity, a topic that has been the subject of intense political and social debate over the past 15 years. While prior literature has primarily focused on the demand side, exploring public support for EU solidarity, the role of political parties in shaping this discourse has received little attention. Our study seeks to address this gap by examining the dynamics of party communication on EU solidarity during times of crisis and relative calm. Specifically, we explore two dimensions of solidarity communication: when parties emphasize solidarity (solidarity when) and which parties are more likely to do so (solidarity by whom). To this end, we analyze a unique dataset of party press releases collected over the past decade in six EU member states. Our findings suggest that the nature of a crisis (symmetric vs. asymmetric) as well as a party’s incumbency status (government vs. opposition) and sociocultural ideology (GAL vs. TAN) play a critical role in shaping party communication on EU solidarity. These findings are relevant retrospectively in terms of understanding policy decisions taken, but also with an eye on a potential path toward a more social union.},
